**Dependency inversion** has two parts: high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules (both should depend on abstractions), and abstractions should not depend on details. The key move is **who owns the interface**: the policy side defines the port in its own terms, and the infrastructure implements it.

## Without and with inversion

```ts
// Without: policy depends directly on a vendor SDK
class BillingService {
  charge(order: Order) {
    new VendorPayClient(process.env.KEY).createCharge(order.total.cents);
  }
}

// With: policy depends on a port it owns
interface PaymentGateway {
  charge(amount: Money, reference: string): Promise<ChargeResult>;
}

class BillingService {
  constructor(private gateway: PaymentGateway) {}
  async charge(order: Order) {
    return this.gateway.charge(order.total, order.id);
  }
}

class VendorPayGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* adapts the vendor SDK */ }
```

{{diagram:dip}}

## Dependency inversion versus injection

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Dependency inversion | A design principle about the direction of source-code dependencies |
| Dependency injection | A technique for supplying the implementation from outside, for example through a constructor |
| IoC container | A tool that automates injection |

You can inject dependencies without inverting them (injecting a concrete class), and you can invert without a container.

## When it is worth it

- External systems you may replace or must fake in tests: payments, email, storage.
- Volatile details (clocks, random numbers, third-party SDKs).
- Core domain logic that should be testable without a database or network.

## When it is not

- Stable, in-process utilities that will never vary.
- One-off scripts and simple CRUD.
- When the interface mirrors the vendor API one to one; then the abstraction leaks and hides nothing.

## Common mistakes

- Interfaces for every class "just in case", leaving a codebase full of single-implementation interfaces.
- Defining the port in the infrastructure package, so the dependency still points the wrong way.
- Letting vendor concepts (error codes, models) leak through the port.

Both are **structural** patterns that put a layer between a client and something else, but their intent differs.

## Comparison

| Aspect | Adapter | Facade |
|---|---|---|
| Intent | Make an existing interface fit the one the client expects | Provide a simpler entry point to a complex subsystem |
| Interface | Matches an interface that already exists (the target) | Defines a new, simplified interface |
| Wraps | Usually one class or service | Several classes or services |
| Typical use | Third-party SDK, legacy API, different data format | Orchestrating several calls behind one operation |
| Client awareness | Client sees the target interface | Client sees only the facade |

{{diagram:compare}}

## Examples (hypothetical)

```ts
// Adapter: our port, their SDK
interface EmailSender { send(to: string, subject: string, body: string): Promise<void> }

class VendorEmailAdapter implements EmailSender {
  constructor(private client: VendorMailClient) {}
  send(to: string, subject: string, body: string) {
    return this.client.deliver({ recipients: [to], title: subject, html: body });
  }
}

// Facade: one call hides several subsystem steps
class CheckoutFacade {
  constructor(private cart: Cart, private pricing: Pricing, private pay: Payments, private ship: Shipping) {}
  async placeOrder(cartId: string) {
    const total = await this.pricing.total(cartId);
    await this.pay.charge(total);
    return this.ship.schedule(cartId);
  }
}
```

## Where each is used at architecture level

- **Adapter** at integration edges, together with an anti-corruption layer when a whole external model must be translated.
- **Facade** for a module's public API in a modular monolith, or a backend-for-frontend that hides several services.

## Common mistakes

- Calling every wrapper an adapter; ask whether it converts to a required interface.
- A facade that grows business logic of its own and becomes a god class.
- Letting vendor types leak through an adapter, which defeats it.
- Using a facade to hide a badly designed subsystem instead of fixing it.

Both patterns implement the **same interface as the object they wrap** and forward calls to it. The difference is the reason for wrapping.

## Comparison

| Aspect | Decorator | Proxy |
|---|---|---|
| Intent | Add responsibilities (logging, caching, retry, metrics) | Control access or represent the object (lazy load, remote call, authorization, rate limit) |
| Stacking | Designed to be layered in any order | Usually a single proxy in front of the real subject |
| Who creates the wrapped object | Client passes it in | Proxy often creates or locates it itself |
| Awareness | Client chooses which decorators to apply | Client often does not know it is talking to a proxy |

{{diagram:wrap}}

## Examples (hypothetical)

```ts
interface ReportService { generate(id: string): Promise<Report> }

// Decorator: adds timing, same contract, stackable
class TimedReportService implements ReportService {
  constructor(private inner: ReportService) {}
  async generate(id: string) {
    const start = Date.now();
    try { return await this.inner.generate(id); }
    finally { console.log('generate took', Date.now() - start, 'ms'); }
  }
}

// Proxy: controls access
class AuthorizedReportService implements ReportService {
  constructor(private inner: ReportService, private canView: (id: string) => boolean) {}
  async generate(id: string) {
    if (!this.canView(id)) throw new Error('Forbidden');
    return this.inner.generate(id);
  }
}

const service = new TimedReportService(new AuthorizedReportService(real, canView));
```

## Overlap in practice

A caching wrapper can be described either way: as a decorator adding caching, or as a caching proxy controlling access to a slow source. Frameworks use proxies heavily for transactions, security and lazy loading, and middleware pipelines are decorator-like. Naming matters less than being clear about the intent.

## Common mistakes

- Putting business rules in a decorator, hiding important behaviour in wrappers.
- Wrapping order surprises: authorization after caching can leak cached data to the wrong user.
- A proxy that changes the contract (new exceptions, different semantics), which violates substitutability.

Domain-driven design separates concepts that are **tracked by identity** from those that are **just values**.

## Comparison

| Aspect | Entity | Value Object |
|---|---|---|
| Identity | Has a unique identity that persists | No identity; defined by its attributes |
| Equality | Same ID means same entity | Same values mean equal |
| Mutability | State changes over its life | Typically immutable; replace to change |
| Examples | Customer, Order, Invoice | Money, Address, DateRange, EmailAddress |
| Lifecycle | Created, changed, archived | Created and discarded freely |

## How to decide

Ask: "If two of these have identical attributes, do I care which one is which?" Two `Money` objects of 10 USD are interchangeable, so Money is a value object. Two orders with the same lines and total are still different orders, so Order is an entity. Context matters: an address is a value object for a shipping label, but may be an entity in a property-management system where a particular building has its own history.

## Example

```ts
class Money {
  private constructor(readonly amount: number, readonly currency: string) {}
  static of(amount: number, currency: string) {
    if (!Number.isInteger(amount)) throw new Error('Use minor units');
    return new Money(amount, currency);
  }
  add(other: Money): Money {
    if (other.currency !== this.currency) throw new Error('Currency mismatch');
    return new Money(this.amount + other.amount, this.currency);
  }
  equals(o: Money) { return o.amount === this.amount && o.currency === this.currency; }
}

class Order {            // entity
  constructor(readonly id: string, private total: Money) {}
}
```

## Benefits of value objects

- Validation happens once, in the constructor, so invalid states cannot exist.
- Immutability removes aliasing bugs and makes them safe to share.
- Behaviour such as `add` or `overlaps` lives with the data, avoiding primitive obsession (strings and numbers passed everywhere).

## Common mistakes

- Making everything an entity with a database ID by default.
- Mutable value objects.
- Using raw strings for email, currency or IDs, so nothing enforces rules.
- Treating the database table as the deciding factor; value objects can be embedded columns or serialised.

An **aggregate** is a cluster of entities and value objects that must stay consistent together. The **aggregate root** is the one entity designated as its gateway. External code interacts only with the root, never with inner objects directly.

## What the root does

- **Enforces invariants**: every state change passes through its methods, so rules cannot be bypassed.
- **Owns identity**: outside references use the root's ID, not inner objects.
- **Defines the transaction boundary**: one aggregate is saved per transaction.
- **Is the unit of loading**: a repository loads and saves the whole aggregate by the root.
- **May emit domain events** describing what happened.

{{diagram:root}}

## Example

```ts
class Invoice {                       // aggregate root
  private lines: InvoiceLine[] = [];
  private status: 'DRAFT' | 'ISSUED' = 'DRAFT';

  addLine(desc: string, amount: Money) {
    if (this.status === 'ISSUED') throw new Error('Cannot change an issued invoice');
    this.lines.push(new InvoiceLine(desc, amount));
  }
  issue() {
    if (this.lines.length === 0) throw new Error('Invoice needs at least one line');
    this.status = 'ISSUED';
  }
}
```

Callers cannot reach into `lines` and edit one; they must call `addLine`. The rules "no changes after issue" and "at least one line to issue" are enforced in one place.

## Rules of thumb

| Rule | Reason |
|---|---|
| Other aggregates hold only the root's ID | Keeps boundaries clear and loads small |
| One repository per aggregate root | Persistence matches the consistency boundary |
| Do not expose collections for external mutation | Prevents bypassing the root |
| Modify one aggregate per transaction where possible | Reduces contention; use events across aggregates |

## Common mistakes

- Anemic roots: public getters and setters with all rules in services, so nothing is protected.
- Huge roots that load an entire object graph.
- Exposing inner entities through the repository, so they get changed directly.
- Choosing roots by table structure rather than by invariants.
